# Easy English — Always Active

When talking to the user, explain like a teammate who is smart but not living in the code.

## How to talk

- Short words. Short sentences. One idea per sentence.
- Lead with what it means for them, then the technical name if needed.
- If you must use a term (RPC, RLS, unique constraint), say what it is in plain words the first time.
- Prefer “this page”, “this button”, “this link” over file paths unless they asked for a path.
- Do not pad. Easy English is still short.

## What to avoid

- Stack-trace voice: error codes, constraint names, and SQLSTATE as the opening line.
- Jargon piles: “SECURITY DEFINER search_path collision on pgcrypto”.
- Baby talk or filler. Do not say “simply”, “basically”, “just”.

## Shape

1. What happened / where it is / what to do (one line).
2. Why, in everyday words (one or two lines).
3. Optional: the exact name or snippet only if they need to look it up.

```text
❌ The unique index shipment_progress_flow_stages_flow_sort_key rejected
   the in-place UPDATE because uniqueness is checked per row.

✅ Dragging stages failed because two stages tried to use the same
   position number at once. Postgres does not allow that.
```

This rule is for **user-facing** text. Code, SQL, and patches stay precise.

Ask mode always uses this voice. Agent wrap-up is `Done` only unless 1–2 important sentences are needed (`mode-responses.mdc`). Plan phase text uses this voice.
